  "summary": "In this letter dated 23 January 1963, Prof. N. G. Ranga writes to C. Rajagopalachari about parliamentary handling of the Colombo proposals concerning the China border dispute. Ranga argues that Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru has been unable to secure a parliamentary decision in favour of accepting the proposals because Congress ministers and legislators fear that a vote would divide Parliament and damage the atmosphere of national unity created by a unanimous November 14 resolution. Ranga insists that Nehru himself has not manoeuvred to avoid the vote; rather, Congress colleagues have persuaded him not to seek a bare majority.",
    "Ranga explains that the Opposition's preferred tactic was to have the issue debated without forcing a vote, thereby demonstrating that Parliament could not give positive support to the government's position and preserving uncertainty about the proposals' final form. He describes coordination—and some difficulty coordinating—with other Opposition groups, including the Jan Sangh and the P.S.P., while noting that opposition leaders generally favour total rejection of the Colombo proposals, apart from the Communists. He concludes that the central achievement is that the government is not asking Parliament to accept the proposals, leaving Parliament free to decide later if its verdict is sought.",
