        "Ranga attributes the absence of a parliamentary vote on the Colombo proposals to Congress's fear of dividing Parliament, rather than to Nehru's manoeuvring.",
        "He presents a no-vote debate as a way to show that Parliament cannot give positive support to the government's acceptance of the proposals.",
        "He links uncertainty over the proposals' final form to Western confidence in Parliament's ability to sustain a belligerent stance against China.",
        "Ranga describes coordination problems among Opposition parties and his reluctance to break a developing understanding with them.",
        "He reports that Opposition leaders broadly support total rejection of the Colombo proposals, while excluding the Communists from that position.",
        "He regards the government's decision not to seek parliamentary acceptance as the most important practical outcome."
